@meta
id: 14-15
title: Swap the columns with backreferences
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-backref
par: 58
minutes: 7
vscode: a regex replace with $1 and $2, if you remember the syntax
open: seed.sql

@brief
The customers insert declares its columns as `(email, id)`, and every
one of the ten rows was written the other way round, id first. The
seed loads without an error and then every customer's email address
is `cus_1001`. Swap the two values on each row.

Chapter 13 gave you groups: `\(…\)` in magic mode, `(…)` in very
magic. What it did not show you is that the replacement side can refer
back to them. `\1` is whatever the first group matched, `\2` the
second. Put them in the replacement in the other order and the swap is
one command.

There is a second insert below, for countries, whose rows have the
same shape and are correct. Make sure your pattern or your range keeps
it out.

@keys
| `\(…\)` or `\v(…)` | a group in the pattern |
| `\1` `\2` | in the replacement: what group 1, group 2 matched |
| `[^']*` | anything but a quote — the contents of a quoted value |
| `:%s/\v'(a)', '(b)'/'\2', '\1'/` | the shape of a swap |

@hints
Capture each quoted value in its own group. The replacement writes
them back in the other order, quotes and all.
--
`\v\('([^']*)', '([^']*)'\)` captures both values of a row. But the
countries rows match that too. Either narrow the range to lines 4 to
13 or make the first group only match a customer id: `(cus_\d+)`.
--
`:%s/\v\('(cus_\d+)', '([^']*)'\)/('\2', '\1')/` and Enter. In very
magic the literal parentheses are `\(` and `\)`, and the bare ones are
the groups.

@solution
    :%s/\v\('(cus_\d+)', '([^']*)'\)/('\2', '\1')/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-one keystrokes to restructure ten lines, and the countries block was
never in danger because the pattern insists on `cus_`.

Backreferences are what make a substitute a restructuring tool rather
than a find-and-replace. The pattern pulls the line apart into named
pieces, the replacement puts the pieces back in a new arrangement, and
anything you write between the references is new text. Swapping two
fields is the classic case; the same shape turns `key=value` into
`key: 'value'`, or `expect(a).toEqual(b)` into `assert.equal(a, b)`.

Two things to notice about the pattern. `[^']*` is the honest way to
say "the contents of a quoted string" — `.*` would be greedy and run
to the last quote on the line. And the choice between narrowing the
range and narrowing the pattern is a real choice: `:4,13s` would have
been shorter to type here, and `cus_\d+` is safer if the file grows a
row. When both are available, prefer the one that says what you mean.

@start seed.sql
-- Customer and country seed data for the integration tests.

INSERT INTO customers (email, id) VALUES
  ('cus_1001', 'lena@example.com'),
  ('cus_1002', 'marc@example.com'),
  ('cus_1003', 'priya@example.com'),
  ('cus_1004', 'tomas@example.com'),
  ('cus_1005', 'aisha@example.com'),
  ('cus_1006', 'jonas@example.com'),
  ('cus_1007', 'mei@example.com'),
  ('cus_1008', 'diego@example.com'),
  ('cus_1009', 'hanna@example.com'),
  ('cus_1010', 'sofia@example.com');

INSERT INTO countries (code, name) VALUES
  ('GB', 'United Kingdom'),
  ('DE', 'Germany'),
  ('FR', 'France'),
  ('NL', 'Netherlands');

@expected seed.sql
-- Customer and country seed data for the integration tests.

INSERT INTO customers (email, id) VALUES
  ('lena@example.com', 'cus_1001'),
  ('marc@example.com', 'cus_1002'),
  ('priya@example.com', 'cus_1003'),
  ('tomas@example.com', 'cus_1004'),
  ('aisha@example.com', 'cus_1005'),
  ('jonas@example.com', 'cus_1006'),
  ('mei@example.com', 'cus_1007'),
  ('diego@example.com', 'cus_1008'),
  ('hanna@example.com', 'cus_1009'),
  ('sofia@example.com', 'cus_1010');

INSERT INTO countries (code, name) VALUES
  ('GB', 'United Kingdom'),
  ('DE', 'Germany'),
  ('FR', 'France'),
  ('NL', 'Netherlands');
